{"shortDescription":"Bill Ackman, CEO of Pershing Square, is questioning whether the Federal Reserve’s traditional approach to fighting inflation remains effective amid an unprecedented surge in demand for artificial intelligence and energy.“The presumption that the Fed raising short-term rates reduces inflation is pre…","astDescription":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"p","children":["Bill Ackman, CEO of Pershing Square, is questioning whether the Federal Reserve’s traditional approach to fighting inflation remains effective amid an unprecedented surge in demand for artificial intelligence and energy."]},{"type":"p","children":["“The presumption that the Fed raising short-term rates reduces inflation is predicated on the belief that higher rates reduce demand and investment,” Ackman stated."]},{"type":"p","children":["However, Ackman argued that assumption may not hold in the current economic environment, where demand for computing power and energy could remain strong regardless of borrowing costs."]},{"type":"p","children":["“What if higher rates don’t reduce demand and investment because the demand for intelligence and energy is unaffected by higher rates?” he asked, citing the potentially enormous returns associated with winning the race for advanced AI."]},{"type":"p","children":["Ackman also warned that higher interest costs could become embedded throughout the economy, potentially creating additional inflationary pressure."]},{"type":"p","children":["“The problem is compounded as the more the Fed raises rates, the more inflation we will have,” he said, suggesting policymakers could face a cycle in which additional tightening generates further price pressures."]},{"type":"p","children":["Ackman ultimately questioned whether conventional economic models apply to the current environment, adding, “I think the Fed might have just made a mistake. Am I right or am I wrong?”"]},{"type":"p","children":[{"type":"b","children":["Fixed Income Funds: "]},{"type":"symbo